/vendor/
node_modules/
/build/
/.phpunit.cache/
/.phpunit.result.cache
/tests/_output/
*.log
# ...but the evidence logs docs/phase-1-report.md cites are deliverables, at
# any depth: the gate and accessibility runs write into subdirectories, and a
# log that is cited as evidence but never committed is not evidence.
!/docs/evidence/*.log
!/docs/evidence/**/*.log
.env
.DS_Store
/docs/evidence/screenshots/*.tmp

# dist/ is a DELIVERABLE (ZIP + SHA256SUMS + source archive) and is committed
# on purpose; see docs/phase-1-report.md §4.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary evidence and the two large archives live in RELEASE ATTACHMENTS, not
# in git. `docs/evidence-manifest.txt` indexes every one of them with its
# SHA-256, so nothing became unverifiable when it moved: download the
# attachment, check the hash, and the file is the file.
#
# Nothing was deleted and no history was rewritten — the copies already
# committed are still in the history they were committed to. This governs
# what FUTURE commits carry, on a repository whose .git had reached 1.4 GB
# and which GitHub already warned about on every push.
#
# The installable ZIP deliberately stays tracked: it is small, it is
# byte-reproducible, and tools/build.sh refuses to overwrite a delivered one
# — a guard that only works while the bytes are somewhere it can check.
docs/evidence/**/*.png
docs/evidence/**/*.jpg
docs/evidence/**/*.jpeg
docs/evidence/**/*.webp
docs/evidence/**/*.gif
docs/evidence/**/*.pdf
docs/evidence/**/*.webm
docs/evidence/**/*.mp4
dist/*-source-*.tar.gz
dist/*-evidence-*.tar.gz
dist/*-reviewable-*.tar.gz
# …that last one, the reviewable bundle, for a different reason: every byte inside it is
# already in git (text evidence, tests, sources, tools). Committing the
# tarball would carry a second copy of files the repository already has, once
# per release. `bash tools/reviewable-bundle.sh` rebuilds it, and the manifest
# records the hash of the one that was sent.
